From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] HFR VRR: each eye's GUI at full 1080p

HFR VRR drew the GUI once, the same for both eyes, so nothing in it had
depth: the subtitle depth setting, the 3D effect strength of the skin
and a Blu-ray's own subtitle depth were all ignored.

It now draws each eye's GUI, as frame packing does: a surface twice the
GUI's height, the left eye above the right, each a full 1080p picture.
With osd_fa_3d at 2 the kernel scans out the half of the eye the video
layer shows on each frame; with the video in 2D it shows the left one.
The plane is not scaled and no eye loses resolution.

The GUI was doubled and osd_fa_3d set to 2 whenever the stereo mode was HFR
VRR, also in the menus and for a 2D film, where frame-sequential 3D is not on;
and the parameter was written only while 3D was supported or on, so after a
failed entry it stayed at 2 under a GUI of normal height. The stacking is now
the window system's decision, made when it knows whether 3D is on: the AML
display sets HFR VRR up with the GUI per eye (the plane's source is the
stacked surface), and the window creates the surface of twice the height and
reports the per-eye size only while 3D is on after the switch. Otherwise the
GUI is drawn once, as in 18_01, with no subtitle depth. A 4K resolution with
GUI scaling off no longer makes a 3840x4320 surface: each eye's GUI is at
most 1920x1080, the output's size, so the surface is at most 1920x2160.

osd_fa_3d is written just before the first flip of a surface of the other
height, 2 for a stacked one and 0 otherwise, wherever the parameter exists:
written at the mode switch, it took effect while the old surface was still
shown (entering: 2 with the 1080 surface; leaving: 0 with the 2160 one) and
a few frames were stretched or shown stacked. With the kernel's latch the
value matches the surface it is committed with. Leaving at shutdown and the
cleanup after a Kodi that died in 3D set it to 0 whatever state they find,
and a non-zero osd_fa_3d is one of the signs that cleanup looks for.

With the viewer's left/right swap (Invert) the video's eyes swap but the GUI's
did not, so subtitle and GUI depth came out reversed; the 18_02 help text
sends users to Invert to fix the glasses' timing. The kernel shows, on each
frame, the GUI half of the eye it reports (fa_eye_view = frame XOR
fa_swap_eyes), and the picture on that frame is the reported eye's for a
plain source and the other eye's under Invert: for SBS/TAB fa_swap_eyes is
right-first XOR Invert, so the right-first part cancels; for MVC
fa_swap_eyes stays 0 and Invert swaps the decoder's view order. So the GUI
follows Invert alone: with it the right eye's GUI is drawn in the upper half.
A right-eye-first source stays a matter for the video layer only.

With the HDR GUI composite the composite drew the stacked FBO through the
upper half's viewport and cleared only that half, and the HDR subtitles were
drawn once, with no eye. The clear and the composite now cover the whole
surface, and in a stacked GUI the HDR subtitles are drawn for each eye.

Shared code, all of it needed because the GUI's size and layout come from
CGraphicContext: two CWinSystemBase hooks, StereoGuiDoubled (per-eye size, or
false) and StereoGuiEyesSwapped, both false by default; GraphicContext places
the right eye below (above when swapped) only while the GUI is stacked, gives
the stacked GUI the per-eye size in GetResInfo, and reads its size again after
a switch, since the window system decides it then; the render loop draws both
eyes in HFR VRR only while the GUI is stacked; subtitle depth is 0 for a GUI
drawn once; the render manager draws the HDR subtitles per eye in a stacked
GUI. A packed stereo subtitle is sized against one eye of the video.

A window creation that fails clears the stacked-GUI state first, so the GUI
is never reported stacked without a stacked surface.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
